ng from whole  slide imaging systems to  immunohistochemistry tests or IHC tests  and nucleic acid-based tests for some  oncology indications so today I'll  provide a regulatory perspective on  molecular diagnostic devices with  particular focus on companion  diagnostics and next-generation  sequencing based on ecology panels  before I begin I would just like to note  that the information presented today are  only intended for summary and discussion  purposes if there are specific questions  about any of the examples that I talked  about today about the specific devices  please refer to the decision summaries  and the summaries of safety and  effectiveness data or SS EDS  which are publicly available documents  on the FDA website and at the last slide  I'll have some a link to the medical  device database where this information  can be accessed today I'll start by  providing an overview of the regulation  of in vitro diagnostic devices or IV DS  then I'll touch on some assay  performance considerations and discuss  how we evaluate IV D products then I'll  move to companion diagnostics providing  an overview and discussing some key  issues in Co development and then I'll  move beyond companion diagnostics and  briefly talk about follow-on companion  diagnostics and complementary  diagnostics and then I'll end by  discussing NGS or next-generation  sequencing oncology panels I'll talk  about the validation considerations for  these types of  tests and also provide some examples of  recent FDA authorizations that have  significantly impacted the current  regulatory paradigm for these types of  devices so what is an IV D in the Code  of Federal Regulations or the CFR which  is basically the set of rules and  regulations by which we abide an IV D is  defined as reagents instruments and  systems intended for use in diagnosis of  disease or other conditions including a  determination of the state of health in  order to cure mitigate treat or prevent  disease or it's equally such products  are intended for use in the collection  preparation and examination of specimens  taken from the human body and this  definition can be found in 21 CFR part  eight oh nine point three when the  agency reviews an IV D we rely on valid  scientific evidence in order to provide  reasonable assurance that the device is  safe and effective for its intended use  so with regard to safety the central  question is really are there reasonable  assurances based on valid scientific  evidence that the probable benefits to  health from use of the device will  outweigh any probable risks with regard  to effectiveness similar question is are  there reasonable assurances again based  on valid scientific evidence that the  use of the device in the target  population will provide clinically  significant results so within the  regulatory framework when evaluating the  safety and effectiveness of the devices  the devices are actually classified  based on a risk based classification  system so there are three regulatory  classes for devices class 1 class 2 and  class 3 and there are differing levels  of controls for each of these classes  where the current level of control will  increase from class 1 to class 3  class 1 devices are considered to be low  risk these devices are subject to  general controls class two devices are  moderate risk and they are subject to  both general controls as well as special  controls and class 3 devices are the  highest risk category and for these both  general and special controls alone are  not sufficient to mitigate the risks  that are associated with the devices for  their intended uses so the risk  classification is wholly dependent upon  the intended use of the device and this  is basically the manner in which the  device is intended to be used so the  intended use is a statement that will  specify the analytes that are being  measured the specimen type that's being  tested as well as providing the context  in which the device should be used so  for example if there's a clinical claim  for prognosis or diagnosis or monitoring  that will be included in the intended  use statement as mentioned this is a  risk based classification and so for IV  DS it's really the risk that is  associated with obtaining false results  from that test and it's the level of  risk that is posed to the patients and  the users of that device the  classification is not based on  technology so even if you have a very  complex technological assay this alone  will not automatically place the device  in the high-risk category however the  technology or the methodology will  influence the types of validation  studies that will be needed to  demonstrate the safety and effectiveness  of the product  so within the regulatory framework the  classification of the device will  determine the pre-market regulatory  pathway for that device although all  devices are subject to be labeled  properly to be manufactured under a  quality assurance program and to have  establishment registration and device  listing forms on file with the FDA but  each class here has a different  regulatory path for example most class 1  low-risk devices are exempt from  premarket notification that is a 510 K  application is not required for those  devices however as I mentioned those  class 1 devices are still subject to  registration and listing class 2 devices  most of them will require a 510 K  submission in order to obtain FDA  clearance and they obtain clearance by  demonstrating that the device is  substantially equivalent to a legally  marketed predicate device in some cases  the class 2 devices may be exempt from  premarket notification and in other  cases there may be an absence of a  predicate device where that device type  has not previously been classified and  in that case then a de novo request for  classification can be submitted for that  device and once authorized that device  can then serve as a predicate for  subsequent devices of the same device  type and then for class 3 high-risk  devices these are subject to a PMA  application to be submitted for FDA  approval so what do we look at when we  review of these medical devices listed  here are the elements of FDA review the  intended use  the indications for use as discussed in  the previous slide are very central to  our review we also look at the  performance of the device which is  comprised of the analytical validation  and the clinical validation when  applicable the instrument and the  software validation is also reviewed we  also look at the labeling and the  labeling for the device must be  compliant with CFR eight or nine point  ten and as I mentioned previously all  devices must be manufactured under a  quality assurance program and they must  meet the quality system regulation  however for PMA applications the quality  system regulation information is  reviewed in the pre market setting so  I'll delve a little bit further into the  scientific review as I mentioned this is  comprised of both the analytical and the  clinical performance the analytical  performance should really demonstrate  the reliability and the accuracy of the  analyte measurements detected by that  test the studies can be specific to that  assay methodology as well as the  intended use and an example of this is  for IHC or immunohistochemistry assays  reader studies are needed  however from molecular nucleic  acid-based tests reader studies aren't  needed but accuracy studies are required  with regard to clinical performance the  test should be correlated with a  clinically meaningful and clinically  significant outcome and some measures to  demonstrate this can be clinical  sensitivity and specificity or positive  and negative predictive values as  appropriate for a few considerations for  analytical performance the test is  really considered as the whole system so  the validation should encompass all the  way from specimen collection and  preparation all the way through to  result reporting and therefore this  includes the pre and a little  steps the validation studies should also  be conducted with the intended specimen  type by using clinical specimens from  the intended population it's also  important to evaluate the performance of  the device across the entire reportable  and measuring range for that device and  this includes around the clinical  cut-offs and importantly the studies  should follow the protocol that will be  in the final device labeling for  clinical performance it should be  determined how the device will be used  in the clinical setting and then the  study design should ensure that it is  appropriate in order to support the  intended use of the device the clinical  study as well as the statistical  analysis plan should be pre specified  and clinical performance should be  established by comparing to an endpoint  or an appropriate surrogate and  typically analytical validation will  precede clinical validation now we'll  move on to companion diagnostics  companion diagnostics are a subset of IV  DS the definition of a companion  diagnostic is that it's a test that  provides information that is essential  for the safe and effective use of a  corresponding therapeutic because the  use of the companion diagnostic and the  corresponding therapeutic are  interdependent the use of the companion  along with the drug is specified in the  labeling of both the diagnostic as well  as the corresponding therapeutic it's  expected that there is contemporaneous  regulatory approvals for both the device  and the corresponding therapeutic  therefore when we review an IV D  application for a companion diagnostic  the review timeline is tied to the  corresponding application for the  therapeutic  product there are however exceptions  when contemporary knees approval may not  be possible and this may happen if there  is an unmet need or if it's in the best  interest of Public Health to approve a  drug prior to approval of the device in  such cases then the companion diagnostic  approval will be included as a post  marketing commitment on the therapeutic  side so for more information about FDA  expectations for companion diagnostics  you can refer to the final draft  guidance which is titled in-vitro  companion diagnostic devices the final  guidance was issued in August of 2014 in  there the definition of a companion is  provided and various scenarios for use  are described in addition FDA policies  for approval and labeling are outlined  to date there are more than 40 approved  companion diagnostic and therapeutic  combinations all of them except for one  are for oncology indications for 18  approved cancer therapeutics there are  33 approved unique IVD companion  diagnostics and all of them except for  one went through the PMA pathway and  these tests cover a total of 18  different biomarkers for a complete and  up-to-date listing of the cleared and  approved companion diagnostic devices  you can refer to the website shown on  this slide so as I mentioned the use of  a device  the use of a companion diagnostic is  tied to the use of a corresponding  therapeutic product and so there are  several key questions that will arise  regarding Co development first how do  you know that a companion diagnostic  device is needed second when you use a  test to identify a biomarker in  the clinical trial for the therapeutic  product will an investigational device  exemption or IDE application be needed  and third what if the final device was  not used in the therapeutic clinical  trial and finally how can a test receive  the same companion diagnostic claim as  the originally approved companion  diagnostic so I'll answer each one of  these questions in the subsequent slides  so first about the need for a companion  diagnostic typically both the  therapeutic and the device are studied  in the same clinical trial  therefore the clinical validation of the  device is supported by the results of  the therapeutic trial in other words the  results from the primary efficacy  population that are used to support  approval of the drug that is the  population that will also be used to  support device approval so when you use  a device in a clinical trial it's very  important that that assay is fully  specified and is locked down prior to  its use in that trial and what I mean by  that is that a specific assays should be  identified for detecting the marker in  that trial the specific protocol should  be identified and the clinical decision  points that will be used to define  biomarker positivity versus biomarker  negativity should be specified in  addition the specific specimen type  should be identified it's also important  that the assay is adequately validated  prior to its use in the trial so  ultimately the decision about the need  for a companion diagnostic is made by  the therapeutic review division but they  make this decision in consultation with  the device center so for these companion  diagnostics they really take a great  deal of collaboration and coordination  between the respective review centers as  well as between the respective device  and drug sponsors  so when thinking about whether companion  diagnostic is needed the central  questions are really is there adequate  evidence of clinical activity of the  therapeutic in the biomarker selected  population as identified by the  companion diagnostic and most  importantly is the test essential for  the safe and effective use of the  corresponding therapeutic product if the  answer to this is yes then undoubtedly  a companion diagnostic will be needed so  what about the need for an IDE  application an approved IDE permits the  device to be shipped lawfully in order  to conduct investigations of the device  without complying with other  requirements of the Food Drug and  Cosmetic Act that would apply to devices  in commercial distribution so typically  when a device is used in a clinical  trial its be it has not been approved  for that indication to be legally  marketed and so an IDE provides for an  exemption for use of that device and the  trial so when we look at all device  investigations some of them may be  subject to the IDE regulation while  others may be exempt from the IDE  regulation of those that are subject to  the IDE regulation some are considered  to be significant risk and therefore  subject to the full IDE requirements  while others may be non significant risk  and although an IDE application does not  need to be submitted to the agency for  these NSR studies they are subject to  the abbreviated IDE requirements which  can be found in 21 CFR part 812 so the  determination about the need for an IDE  is not a benefit risk decision it's  really an assessment of risk that is  posed to the patients who are enrolled  into that trial so the question is does  use of the test result pose a  significant risk if the answer is yes  and most likely that trial will be  use of the device in that trial will be  considered to be significant risk and an  IDE application will be needed in order  to determine if an IDE is needed for a  specific trial we recommend that a study  risk determination pre submission is  submitted to the Center for Devices so  the third question was about what  happens if the clinical trial assay or  CTA differs from the companion  diagnostic so one example of when this  might happen is if a prototype of an  assay is used to enroll patients in the  trial and then the assay goes through  some optimization or some changes and  then a different assay will come forward  for marketing authorization in such a  case changing the test can lead to  changes in the assay performance and  this in turn can change the patient  population from what was selected in the  therapeutic clinical trial when this  happens  bridging studies are required in order  to perform a bridging study patient  specimens that tested both CTA negative  and CTA positive should be retained and  banked so that they can be retested with  the final companion diagnostic device in  such a case there should be no  collection or selection bias and  therefore you should ensure that the  samples that are stored there and their  integrity is maintained and one way to  do this is through demonstration of the  stability of the analytes in those  stored specimens in order to ensure that  the storage duration was acceptable and  the quality of the assay is acceptable  when performing bridging study  statistical analysis plan should  consider potential discordance 'as and  also should account for any missing  samples importantly bridging studies are  not just a method comparison study  bridging studies should evaluate the  concordance between the CTA test results  and the companion diagnostic test  results but it should also demonstrate  that the clinical outcomes that were  correlated with the CTA are maintained  with the test results from the companion  diagnostic device the last question was  about the possibility for the same  companion diagnostic claim as the  original companion diagnostic claim for  a new test it's recognized that often  times samples from the original clinical  trial that's used to support the  approval of the original companion  diagnostic may not exist any longer or  they just may not be available for  retesting in such a case there are two  potential pathways that could be  followed in order to gain the same  companion diagnostic claim the first is  that they can follow the path of the  original companion diagnostic they could  perform a new clinical trial in order to  obtain clinical outcome data that's  correlated with the new test we  recognize that it can be extremely  burdensome and costly to perform a new  clinical trial and so an alternative  path is to get a follow-on companion  diagnostic claim a follow-on companion  diagnostic should consistently and  accurately select the same intended use  patient population as the originally  approved companion diagnostic device and  therefore the can follow on companion  diagnostic should demonstrate the same  or the comparable level of analytical  and clinical performance for the  specific mutations or the specific  analytes that are being tested in the  originally approved companion diagnostic  so in order to demonstrate this  performance it's important that a  clinical concordance study which is  essentially a method comparison  study is performed to compare the new  test with the previously approved  companion diagnostic so a term that has  emerged in the recent past is  complementary Diagnostics and these are  tests that in contrast to companion  diagnostics these are not essential for  the safe and effective use of the  therapeutic product complementary  Diagnostics identify a biomarker to find  subsets of patients who respond  particularly well to a drug and aid in  the benefit risk assessment for  individual patients therefore it is not  a prerequisite for receiving the drug an  example of a situation in which a  complementary diagnostic would arise is  when a drug is approved for an  all-comers however there may be  differential clinical outcomes based on  biomarker status as defined by this  complementary diagnostic and so in such  cases then a complementary diagnostic  may be needed because the complementary  Diagnostics are also studied in the  clinical trial that's used to evaluate  the performance of the therapeutic  product the development path for  complementary Diagnostics is similar to  that for companion diagnostics so now  I'll move on to NGS oncology panels  these next-generation sequencing based  on ecology panels are increasingly being  employed in the clinical setting part of  their advantage is that they have the  ability to screen a very large number of  biomarkers which can be up to thousands  of biomarkers that can be screened  simultaneously therefore the tests  have broad intended uses due to the  sheer volume of the number of sequences  that can be obtained from a single run  validation for such a test on a per  variant or per market basis is not  feasible and therefore these types of  tests have introduced challenges to the  companion diagnostic paradigm which  traditionally has been one test for one  drug as I mentioned previously the  validation studies for a device should  be designed to support the performance  characteristics of that device for its  intended use but as I mentioned there  are challenges one is that it is not  feasible to validate all possible  alterations that may be detected by an  NGS test a second clinical specimens for  analytical validation may be limiting  particularly for rare tumor types or for  tumors in which there are low prevalence  biomarkers and third there is a lack of  reference methods and standards that can  be used in order to demonstrate the  accuracy of these NGS uncle panels so in  the next few slides I'll address each of  these challenges by presenting  strategies that have been implemented by  some of the tests that we've reviewed  first with regard to the inability to  evaluate every single variant that could  be detected by an NGS test it's  recognized that this is terribly  burdensome and therefore we recommend  that all variants with clinical  companion diagnostic claims should be  included in the validation studies  however these NGS tests include can  include many more genes or biomarkers  that extend beyond the markers so  with a companion diagnostic claim so in  such cases we then recommend that a  representative approach can be taken in  order to support the validation of those  other single nucleotide variants and  insertions and deletions or in Dells  when taking a representative approach  it's important that a range of variant  types are included which represent or  give consideration to the sizes of the  different variants as well as the  contexts the genomic context that those  variants can be detected in on the other  hand a representative variant approach  is not acceptable for fusions or gene  rearrangements because the performance  of the device for one gene fusion may  not be able to be extrapolated to  fusions detected in other genes so what  I really mean by this is that if fusions  in the AL gene are used in the  validation studies this cannot be used  in order to gain claims that the test  can detect fusions in other genes such  as rosslyn however within the AL gene we  recommend that the most common the two  or three most common out confusions are  represented in the validation studies in  order to obtain the claim that a test  can detect alkanes and so therefore  every single out fusion that has been  identified does not need to be included  in the validation studies there are also  can be some post-market considerations  in order to obtain a pre market post  market balance and so there may be  circumstances under which post market  data can be provided for some analytical  studies in order to further demonstrate  the performance of the device  regarding the potential lack of clinical  specimens the test performance should be  established with the intended clinical  specimens however in certain analytical  studies it may be appropriate to use  contrived samples provided at an  appropriately designed functional  characterization study is performed so a  contrived sample functional  characterization study basically should  demonstrate that the contrived samples  are able to appropriately mimic the  clinical specimens and therefore can be  used as surrogates in the validation  studies another consideration for pan  tumor claims in which it is not possible  to test every single tumor type that has  been identified in these cases we  recommend that comparability across a  variety of common and challenging tumors  is demonstrated and we typically  recommend that at least 10 tumor types  are represented also because different  tumor types can have different potential  interference it's important to assess  interference effects  an example is melanin in melanoma and  then finally we also recommend that data  is provided regarding the invalid rates  and the performance metrics across tumor  types and this information can be based  on historical data or a retrospective  analysis of data that already exists  regarding the lack of reference methods  in order to demonstrate accuracy a  validated orthogonal method may be used  as a comparator if the method is not an  fda-approved  or an FDA cleared test then minimal  level of validation data is needed for  the comparator and we generally  recommend that at a minimum limit of  and sensitivity studies as well as DNA  input and precision studies are  performed to support the performance of  that other test as a comparator method  to demonstrate accuracy of the test  additional validation considerations are  that the validation should evaluate how  different source errors can affect  device performance  NGS tests are a complex process that  involves numerous steps in the workflow  this encompasses sample preparation  library construction sequencing and data  analysis and even result reporting in  such cases for these types of tests  different error profiles are associated  with different analytes which can range  from parameters including variant type  genomic context and allele frequency so  therefore we recommend that quality  metrics are established at both the  sample and the variant levels and that  these quality metrics should really look  beyond exon level coverage and so some  of the quality metrics that we request  are listed here the reportable range  should be clearly defined and this is  with respect to genomic regions variant  types that will be detected by the assay  as well as the variant allele  frequencies that are detected and all of  the quality control metrics and the  validations should be developed in order  to ensure the performance of the device  within the claimed reportable range in  addition sequencing metrics should be  provided such as the run in the sample  QC mapping metrics which will include  coverage and coverage should cover depth  completeness and uniformity and  additional metrics can be variant  calling as well as reporting metrics so  the validation considerations that I  discussed were implemented  and stemmed from our experiences with  NGS tests that we've reviewed so listed  here are some of the NGS tests actually  all of the NGS tests that we have  reviewed and cleared or approved the  first NGS tests were actually not  companion diagnostic devices those were  approved in November of 2013 and those  were two Illumina tests that were  intended to detect cystic fibrosis  variants that are intended to aid in the  diagnosis of cystic fibrosis as they're  not companion diagnostics and they were  for an aid and diagnosis for cystic  fibrosis those went through the 510 k  pathway the first NGS based companion  diagnostic test was approved in December  of 2016 and that was the foundation  focus CDX bracha test from from  foundation medicine that tests detected  variance in the Braco one and the  bracket two genes and more recently in  June and November of last year for  next-generation sequencing tests were  approved three of these were considered  next-generation sequencing oncology  panels and they're listed here so  basically these tests included genes  beyond those that have companion  diagnostic claims so the first test that  was approved in June of 2017 went  through the PMA pathway and it was for  the angka mine DX target test from  thermo Fisher Scientific the next was  approved in note was cleared in November  of 2017 and that was for the msk impact  test from Memorial sloan-kettering and  this went through the Genova pathway and  therefore it did not have any specific  companion diagnostic claims associated  with it and then finally later in that  same month on November 30th the  foundation 1c DX test was approved the  p.m. a pathway and that test is from  Foundation medicine so these three FDA  authorizations have really shaped the  current regulatory paradigm for NGS  uncle panels and that's shown here so in  the classical companion diagnostic model  where it was one test for one drug  you only had biomarkers with companion  diagnostic claims but with the recent  clearance of the msk impact panel this  really created some additional levels of  biomarkers that are associated with  different levels of evidence and so you  can see that the while I'll refer to  level 1 biomarkers as those with  companion diagnostic claims level 2  biomarkers are those that are considered  to be cancer mutations with evidence of  clinical significance and level 3  biomarkers are the cancer mutations with  potential clinical significance and I'll  talk about the examples here the uncle  mine test and the foundation one CDX  tests all fit into the level 1 category  but they actually had both level 1 2 &amp; 3  biomarkers while the msk impact test  went through a class-2 pathway and had  only level 2 and level 3 biomarkers but  it's important to note that a test  cannot be cleared with only level 3  biomarkers and so level 3 biomarkers are  obtained along with level 2 and/or level  1 biomarker claims so to talk a little  bit more about each of these three tests  the angka my index target test is a lung  cancer panel the tested text single  nucleotide variants and deletions in 23  dreams from DNA as well as fusions in  the Ross 1 gene from RNA that has been  isolated from FFPE tumor tissue samples  from non-small-cell lung cancer patients  it was the first approved test that  simultaneously assesses variants in  different genes in order to determine  patient eligibility for treatment with  multiple non-small cell lung cancer  targeted therapies and therefore it had  multiple companion diagnostic claims  which are listed here  the first is that the test was approved  to detect b-raf v600 mutations for  treatment with depravity and tremendum  it was approved for the detection of  Ross one fusions for treatment with  crizotinib and it also was approved with  a follow-up companion diagnostic claim  to detect EGFR exon 19 deletions and l85  8 our substitution mutations for  treatment which a fitting in and  therefore this test had both level 1  level 2 and level 3 claims for the msk  impact test the intended use is shown  here the assay is a qualitative in vitro  diagnostic test that uses targeted  sequencing of FFPE tumor tissue matched  with normal specimens from patients with  solid malignant neoplasms in order to  detect tumor gene alterations the test  is intended to detect mutations that  include point mutations and small indels  as well as microsatellite instability  and its intended for use by qualified  healthcare professionals in accordance  with professional guidelines importantly  the test is not conclusive or  prescriptive for labeled use of any  specific therapeutic product and  therefore it does not have the level 1  biomarker or companion diagnostic claims  the msk impact test is a solid tumor  panel it identifies mutations in 468  genes as well as microsatellite  as I mentioned this went through the de  novo pathway and it really established a  class-2 pathway for these NGS uncle  panels that have only level 2 and level  3 claims so these are considered to be  class 2 tumor profiling tests and they  are eligible for third party review  third party review is a program that was  implemented in order to approve the  efficiency and the timeliness of the  510k review process for certain device  types under this program there are  accredited third parties who can conduct  the 510k review and they will provide  the review package as well as their  recommendation to the FDA after they  have reviewed the information and so  really devices under this class can go  through the class to pathway either  through the third party review pathway  or they can directly submit a 510k  submission to the FDA and I'd also like  to note that one of the accredited third  party reviewers for this type of test is  New York State Department of Health so  going back to the regulatory paradigm we  have the three levels of review and we  saw based on the uncle mind test that it  had claims for both level for all of the  levels level 1 level 2 and level 3  importantly the validation for level 1  biomarkers which have companion  diagnostic claims require that  analytical validation is performed for  each marker associated with such a  companion diagnostic acclaim the  clinical claims are established through  a clinical study or for follow-on  companion diagnostics through clinical  concordance results  for level two claims analytical  validation can be conducted per marker  or through representative approach for  SMBs and indels in contrast to the level  one the level of evidence for clinical  validation is that it can be established  in professional guidelines but it does  not have to be demonstrated directly  with the test of interest and then for  level three biomarkers and a little cook  validation is supported through the  representative approach while clinical  validation is not demonstrated either in  professional guidelines or with the test  but rather it's suggestive based on  available evidence and that available  available evidence can include mecca's  mechanistic rationale as well as  published literature so for the last NGS  uncle panel that has recently been  approved this was the foundation once  EDX it's a solid tumor panel and the  test was approved to detect  substitutions in der indels which are  insertions and deletions as well as copy  number alterations in 324 genes as well  as select gene rearrangements it was  also approved to detect two genomic  signatures microsatellite instability  and tumor mutation burden the test was  approved for use with 15 targeted  therapies in five different cancer types  and therefore it had companion  diagnostic claims and it's also intended  to provide tumor mutation profiling in  accordance with professional guidelines  and these are level 2 biomarker claims  importantly this test was the first  breakthrough device to complete the PMA  process and it was the second device to  be reviewed and approved under the fda  CMS parallel review program here's an  excerpt a quick snapshot of the intended  use from the device shown here is table  with all of the companion diagnostic  claims for which the device is approved  you can see here the five different  tumor types are not small cell lung  cancer melanoma breast cancer colorectal  cancer and ovarian cancer along with the  associated biomarkers and therapeutics  so as I mentioned this was the second  IVD that was reviewed and approved under  the fda CMS parallel review program this  parallel review program was piloted in  2011 and fully implemented in 2016 and  it's aimed at reducing the time between  FDA approval of a device and CMS  Medicare coverage so on November 30th  when the FDA approved this device on the  same day CMS issued a proposed national  coverage determination or an NC d for  NGS cancer diagnostics for Medicare  beneficiaries after the public comment  period  CMS finalized the NCD on March 16th of  this year for NGS for Medicare  beneficiaries with advanced cancer the  coverage is for diagnostic laboratories  using NGS tests for patients with  advanced cancer and that's defined as  being recurrent metastatic  relapsed/refractory  or advanced cancer as stages 3 &amp; 4 in  order to get coverage the test must have  FDA approval or clearance as a companion  diagnostic it also must have an  fda-approved or cleared indication for  use in the patient's cancer and the  results should be provided to the  treating physician using a report  template to specify the treatment  options the coverage decision also noted  that coverage for other NGS lab tests  for Medicare patients with cancer are  made by