y such products  are intended for use in the collection  preparation and examination of specimens  taken from the human body and this  definition can be found in 21 CFR part  eight oh nine point three when the  agency reviews an IV D we rely on valid  scientific evidence in order to provide  reasonable assurance that the device is  safe and effective for its intended use  so with regard to safety the central  question is really are there reasonable  assurances based on valid scientific  evidence that the probable benefits to  health from use of the device will  outweigh any probable risks with regard  to effectiveness similar question is are  there reasonable assurances again based  on valid scientific evidence that the  use of the device in the target  population will provide clinically  significant results so within the  regulatory framework when evaluating the  safety and effectiveness of the devices  the devices are actually classified  based on a risk based classification  system so there are three regulatory  classes for devices class 1 class 2 and  class 3 and there are differing levels  of controls for each of these classes  where the current level of control will  increase from class 1 to class 3  class 1 devices are considered to be low  risk these devices are subject to  general controls class two devices are  moderate risk and they are subject to  both general controls as well as special  controls and class 3 devices are the  highest risk category and for these both  general and special controls alone are  not sufficient to mitigate the risks  that are associated with the devices for  their intended uses so the risk  classification is wholly dependent upon  the intended use of the device and this  is basically the manner in which the  device is intended to be used so the  intended use is a statement that will  specify the analytes that are being  measured the specimen type that's being  tested as well as providing the context  in which the device should 