ce in  such cases then the companion diagnostic  approval will be included as a post  marketing commitment on the therapeutic  side so for more information about FDA  expectations for companion diagnostics  you can refer to the final draft  guidance which is titled in-vitro  companion diagnostic devices the final  guidance was issued in August of 2014 in  there the definition of a companion is  provided and various scenarios for use  are described in addition FDA policies  for approval and labeling are outlined  to date there are more than 40 approved  companion diagnostic and therapeutic  combinations all of them except for one  are for oncology indications for 18  approved cancer therapeutics there are  33 approved unique IVD companion  diagnostics and all of them except for  one went through the PMA pathway and  these tests cover a total of 18  different biomarkers for a complete and  up-to-date listing of the cleared and  approved companion diagnostic devices  you can refer to the website shown on  this slide so as I mentioned the use of  a device  the use of a companion diagnostic is  tied to the use of a corresponding  therapeutic product and so there are  several key questions that will arise  regarding Co development first how do  you know that a companion diagnostic  device is needed second when you use a  test to identify a biomarker in  the clinical trial for the therapeutic  product will an investigational device  exemption or IDE application be needed  and third what if the final device was  not used in the therapeutic clinical  trial and finally how can a test receive  the same companion diagnostic claim as  the originally approved companion  diagnostic so I'll answer each one of  these questions in the subsequent slides  so first about the need for a companion  diagnostic typically both the  therapeutic and the device are studied  in the same clinical trial  therefore the clinical validation of the  device is supported by the results of  the therapeutic trial in other words the  results from the primary efficacy  population that are used to support  approval of the drug that is the  population that will also be used to  support device approval so when you use  a device in a clinical trial it's very  important that that assay is fully  specified and is locked down prior to  its use in that trial and what I mean by  that is that a specific assays should be  identified for detecting the marker in  that trial the specific protocol should  be identified and the clinical decision  points that will be used to define  biomarker positivity versus biomarker  negativity should be specified in  addition the specific specimen type  should be identified it's also important  that the assay is adequately validated  prior to its use in the trial so  ultimately the decision about the need  for a companion diagnostic is made by  the therapeutic review division but they  make this decision in consultation with  the device center so for these companion  diagnostics they really take a great  deal of collaboration and coordination  between the respective review centers as  well as between the respective device  and drug sponsors  so when thinking about whether companion  diagnostic is needed the central  questions are really is there adequate  evidence of clinical activity of the  therapeutic in the biomarker selected  population as identified by the  companion diagnostic and most  importantly is the test essential for  the safe and effective use of the  corresponding therapeutic product if the  answer to this is yes then undoubtedly  a companion diagnostic will be needed so  what about the need for an IDE  application an approved IDE permits the  device to be shipped lawfully in order  to conduct investigations of the device  without complying with other  requirements of the Food Drug and  Cosmetic Act that would apply to devices  in commercial distribution so typically  when a device is used in a clinical  trial its be it has not be