ubmit a 510k  submission to the FDA and I'd also like  to note that one of the accredited third  party reviewers for this type of test is  New York State Department of Health so  going back to the regulatory paradigm we  have the three levels of review and we  saw based on the uncle mind test that it  had claims for both level for all of the  levels level 1 level 2 and level 3  importantly the validation for level 1  biomarkers which have companion  diagnostic claims require that  analytical validation is performed for  each marker associated with such a  companion diagnostic acclaim the  clinical claims are established through  a clinical study or for follow-on  companion diagnostics through clinical  concordance results  for level two claims analytical  validation can be conducted per marker  or through representative approach for  SMBs and indels in contrast to the level  one the level of evidence for clinical  validation is that it can be established  in professional guidelines but it does  not have to be demonstrated directly  with the test of interest and then for  level three biomarkers and a little cook  validation is supported through the  representative approach while clinical  validation is not demonstrated either in  professional guidelines or with the test  but rather it's suggestive based on  available evidence and that available  available evidence can include mecca's  mechanistic rationale as well as  published literature so for the last NGS  uncle panel that has recently been  approved this was the foundation once  EDX it's a solid tumor panel and the  test was approved to detect  substitutions in der indels which are  insertions and deletions as well as copy  number alterations in 324 genes as well  as select gene rearrangements it was  also approved to detect two genomic  signatures microsatellite instability  and tumor mutation burden the test was  approved for use with 15 targeted  therapies in five different cancer types  and therefore it had companion  diagnostic claims and it's also intended  to provide tumor mutation profiling in  accordance with professional guidelines  and these are level 2 biomarker claims  importantly this test was the first  breakthrough device to complete the PMA  process and it was the second device to  be reviewed and approved under the fda  CMS parallel review program here's an  excerpt a quick snapshot of the intended  use from the device shown here is table  with all of the companion diagnostic  claims for which the device is approved  you can see here the five different  tumor types are not small cell lung  cancer melanoma breast cancer colorectal  cancer and ovarian cancer along with the  associated biomarkers and therapeutics  so as I mentioned this was the second  IVD that was reviewed and approved under  the fda CMS parallel review program this  parallel review program was piloted in  2011 and fully implemented in 2016 and  it's aimed at reducing the time between  FDA approval of a device and CMS  Medicare coverage so on November 30th  when the FDA approved this device on the  same day CMS issued a proposed national  coverage determination or an NC d for  NGS cancer diagnostics for Medicare  beneficiaries after the public comment  period  CMS finalized the NCD on March 16th of  this year for NGS for Medicare  beneficiaries with advanced cancer the  coverage is for diagnostic laboratories  using NGS tests for patients with  advanced cancer and that's defined as  being recurrent metastatic  relapsed/refractory  or advanced cancer as stages 3 &amp; 4 in  order to get coverage the test must have  FDA approval or clearance as a companion  diagnostic it also must have an  fda-approved or cleared indication for  use in the patient's cancer and the  results should be provided to the  treating physician using a report  template to specify the treatment  options the coverage decision also noted  that coverage for other NGS lab tests  for Medicare patients with cancer are  made by local Medicare administrative  ders and for more information about the  decision memorandum on CMS's NCD the  link is provided at the bottom of this  slide so in conclusion I think the  regulatory models for companion  diagnostics and NGS oncology panels have  really evolved over time due to  increasing efforts and personalized  medicine and a lot of these efforts have  really been driven by device innovation  and device advancements and a couple of  takeaways are that these type to bring  these products to market it takes a lot  of collaboration and coordination  between all of the stakeholders for  example for companion diagnostics it  takes coordination communication between  the diagnostic and the drug sponsors as  well as within the agency between the  review centers the respective review  centers also through programs like third  party review and parallel review it  takes a lot of coordination between  agencies as well as with the device  sponsors as well so with that I'd like  to end here and listed here are a few  additional resources to get some  additional information on some of the  topics that I've discussed today and  also if after this session any questions  arise about specific devices or anything  I've talked about today you can please  feel free to send me any questions at  the email address here thank you very  much
 

